  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThis algorithm attempts to improve upon the current record of 0.3814 by using a higher resolution and a combination of various optimization techniques. Unlike the previous method which relies primarily on simulated annealing, this new approach integrates several mathematical and numerical optimization techniques to systematically explore the solution space and strengthen the search for better candidates. The key rationales are:\n\n1. Increase resolution to better approximate the continuous problem (n=400)\n2. Use multiple optimization strategies in parallel: simulated annealing, basin hopping and local hill climbing\n3. Introduce geometric transformations in the search space (projection and inversion)\n4. Maintain several running candidates to avoid local minima\n5. Adjust hyperparameter schedules based on search progression\n6. Include fallback mechanisms for validation and recovery\n\nThe major bottleneck in the previous approach was its single-strategy focus and high parameter sensitivity. This algorithm addresses these by incorporating multiple search strategies and structured hyperparameter management.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nThe problem is defined by:\n- Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Subject to \u222b h(x) dx = 1, 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\nDiscretization:\n- Domain: [0,2] with `n_points = 400`\n- dx = 2.0/400 = 0.005\n- Representation: array z \u2208 [0,1]^{400} with \u2211z = 200 (since n_points/2 = 200)\n\nFeasibility Enforcement:\n- Maintain sum exactly via projection: \n  `h_new = clip(h_old) + deficit adjustment` (derived from scaling projection)\n- Projection function `project(h)`:\n  s_old = original_sum\n  s_target = n_points/2.0\n  if abs(s_old - s_target) > 1e-5: apply correction\n\nObjective Evaluation:\n- `c5 = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx`\n- Always evaluate via discrete correlation\n\nSearch Space:\n- Hyperparameter schedule for: T0, T_end, step_size, move_ratio, penalty_weight\n- Temperature schedules: logarithmic decay\n- Move probabilities: annealed from uniform exploration to focused visits\n- Geometric transformations: inversion and projection\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Setup\n   - Import libraries and initialize seed\n   - Project initial_h_values to n=400 with sum constraint\n   - Initialize multiple candidates with diverse start states\n\n2. Core loop (1100s total)\n   - Maintain active set of best candidates\n   - Cycle through optimization strategies (weighting by time)\n   - Apply recommended transformations at each state\n   - Schedule adaptive exponential decay\n\n3. Optimization strategies:\n   a. Simulated annealing (weight: primary during first 80% budget)\n   b. Basin hopping (weight: increasing after 50% budget)\n   c. Local hill climbing (weight: only after 90% budget)\n   d. Transformation search (always active)\n\n4. Transformation rules:\n   - Inversion: h \u21a6 1-h (with sum adjustment)\n   - Projection: h \u21a6 projections to boundary/interior states\n   - Random initialization: as needed\n\n5. Candidate selection:\n   - Track best candidate by c5 value\n   - Maintain top N=5 candidates throughout\n   - Renew set periodically based on fitness trend\n\n6. Termination:\n   - Target improvement plateau (20 cycle without improvement)\n   - Use fallback candidate if search fails\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n\n- Initial candidates: 3 in total\n  1. Provided parent projection\n  2. Random with sum constraint\n  3. Function approximation from literature\n\n- Strategy weights:\n  Temperature decay influence weights:\n      annealing: 0.4\n      basin: 0.3\n      local: 0.2\n  Transformation: 0.1\n\n- Move settings:\n  Global vs local balance: adaptive between 70% global/sampling and 30% local\n  Temperature multipliers: 0.8 to 0.999\n\n- Initial hyperparameters:\n  T0: 0.05, T_end: 1e-4, step_size: 0.2, move_ratio: 0.75\n\nBudget and performance:\n\n- Estimation: \n  Objective eval: 400*log(400) \u2248 5k operations ~ 0.5s per eval\n  Update manager: 10-15k operations ~ 1s per cycle\n  Total per evaluation: ~0.6-0.7s\n  With 1100s: \u2248 1500-2000 evaluations\n\n- Measurement checkpoints:\n  - After 500s: target final candidate\n  - Annually (every 5 check): record history\n\nValidation:\n\n- Final candidate undergoes comprehensive checks:\n  - Explicit sum enforcement: \u2211h = n/2 exactly\n  - Clipping validation: all values [0,1]\n  - Objective evaluation: via `np.correlate`\n  - Property checks: \n      h.scatter_diagram (if available) or fallback evaluation\n\n- Fallback mechanism:\n  Use best seen candidate if validator fails; use parents if direct eval unavailable\n\nFormal return format:\n(h_values: vector300, c5_value, n_points=400)\n\nBidirectional matching with the objective:\nFor precise comparison, always use:\n   c5 = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx\n\nEven when using fallback, final scoring must exactly match the evaluator.\n\nExecution requires:\nPython 3.8+, numpy, scipy, matplotlib\n\nFull implementation details below.",
